<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdel Hakim Amer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdel Hakim Amer was an Egyptian army officer, a close friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> and a leading member of the Free Officers who seized power in 1952. As commander of the armed forces and field marshal, he served as Nasser's representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a>. Blamed for the defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967, he was arrested over an alleged coup plot and died in custody, officially by suicide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdel-hakim-amer.jpg" alt="Abdel Hakim Amer" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdel_Hakim_Amer,_1955.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amer was born on 11 December 1919 in Samalut, in the governorate of El Minya, into an affluent family. His father was a landowner and village mayor, and his maternal uncle, Mohammed Haidar Pasha, served as minister of war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a>. After school he attended the Egyptian Military Academy, graduated in 1938 and was commissioned into the Egyptian Army in 1939. He served in the 1948 Arab-Israeli War in the same unit as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Amer was one of the leaders of the military coup of 1952 that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> and brought General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-naguib">Muhammad Naguib</a> and Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> to power. The following year he was promoted straight from major to major-general, bypassing four ranks, and made Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>. In 1956 he became commander-in-chief of the joint military command established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and he led Egyptian forces against Israeli and allied British and French forces in the Suez war.</p><p>Afterwards he accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> of provoking an unnecessary war and then blaming the military for the result. As Nasser's representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he was detained by rebels during the coup of 1961 and sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. In October 1959 he had become Syria's governor, holding presidential authority. He later served as Minister for Defense and Vice President, and in March 1964 became first vice-president and deputy supreme commander, with power to rule for 60 days if the president was incapacitated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s defeat by Israel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of June 1967 ended his career. Historians including Hazem Kandil place much of the responsibility on Amer, whose control of the military fitted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>'s policy of making institutions the fiefdoms of those most loyal to him; the war in North Yemen, where nearly half of the Western-trained officer corps were serving, is also seen as a cause. On 6 June, when he heard of the fall of Abu-Ageila, he is said to have panicked and ordered all units in Sinai to retreat, which turned the retreat into a rout. On 17 June he was relieved of all duties and forced into early retirement.</p><p>In August 1967 Amer, over 50 officers and two former ministers, among them Shams Badran, were arrested for allegedly plotting a coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>, and Amer was kept under house arrest at his villa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. He died on 13 September 1967. The official Egyptian position was that he swallowed poison pills when officers arrived to question him. Another version holds that officers gave him the choice of a trial for treason or an honourable death by poison. In September 2012 his family filed a case to investigate his death, claiming that he was murdered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdel Hakim Amer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Political parties were dissolved, including the Ba'ath, and Egyptian officials and security services dominated, with Field Marshal <b>Abdel Hakim Amer</b> as Nasser's representative in Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Astal. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abdel Hakim Amer"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
